    <exercise>
        <title>Conservation of Energy Determines Motion</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the Atwood machine shown in the figure below, with ideal
                ropes and pulleys. Our system of interest will be the masses and the Earth.
                This system is <term>effectively one dimensional</term> because it can be fully described with
                a single variable, <m>z</m>. We will take <m>z</m> to be the displacement from the
                intial position of the mass <m>M</m>, with the positive direction oriented downwards.
            </p>
            <image source="images/hwimg/hw6/atwood.png" width="50%">
                <shortdescription>Image of an Atwood machine</shortdescription>
                <description>
                    <p>
                        Image of an atwood machine. A block of mass <m>m</m> lies on the ground,
                        attached to a vertical rope that goes over a pulley. The rope hangs downwards
                        and attaches to a mass <m>M</m>, which hangs vertically at some distance
                        from the ground.
                    </p>
                </description>
            </image>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find an expression for the potential energy <m>~{U(z)}</m> for this
                    system, setting <m>U(z=0)=0</m>. Meaning: find the change in potential 
                    energy when the masses are displaced by <m>z</m>. Make sure you're using
                    the constraint.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Using the constraint, show that the kinetic energy of this system (ignoring any
                    motion of the Earth, of course), is 
                    <md>
                    K=\frac{1}{2}(M+m)\dot{z}^2    
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The external interaction for this system is the tension forces
                    from the rope. Show that the rope does no net work on the masses,
                    so that we can really say that the mechanical energy <m>K+U</m>
                    remains constant. In fact, <q>ideal rope and pulley system</q>,
                    regardless of context, really means <q>no dissipation</q>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Since there's no dissipation in our system, the total energy
                    E(t) remains cosntant. By setting the time derivative <m>\dfrac{dE}{dt}=0</m>, 
                    show that you can deduce the Atwood machine's equation of motion, which is
                    <md>
                        (m+M)\ddot{z}=(M-m)g \Rightarrow \ddot{z}=\dfrac{M-m}{M+m}g.
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Lesson: if a system has no dissipation and can be described by a single
                    variable <m>q</m>, then the constancy of the mechanical energy completely
                    determines its motion! In a higher level mechanics course, you will learn
                    that this turns out to be true <em>in general</em>, which is quite powerful.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>The Astro-blaster</title>
        <p>
            A tennis ball with a small mass m sits on top of a basketball with a large mass M (see figure).
            The bottom of the basketball is a height <m>h</m> above the ground, and the tennis ball is standing 
            right on top of it. After the balls are dropped, how high does the tennis ball go? 
            Assume <m>M\gg m</m> and that the balls are separated by a very small distance, so that they basically
            collide the moment the basketball reverses its motion. No need to solve the elastic collision problem
            again from scracth. If you're up to the challenge, imagine what would happen if you have N balls 
            stacked on top of each other, all satisfying <m>m_1\gg m_2 \gg m_3 \gg \cdots \gg m_N</m> [this is optional].
        </p>
        
        <image source="images/hwimg/hw6/astro-blaster.svg" width="40%">
            <shortdescription>A small ball is on top of a large ball. The large ball is at
                a certain height above the ground.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
        <title>The Rocket-Powered Skateboard</title>
        <p>
            Consider a rocket-powered skateboard that is about to roll downhill. To escape on the right with 
            the maximum speed, where should it fire a brief impulse (i.e., blast its engines)?
            <fn>This problem is adapted from <url href="https://doi.org/10.1119/1.5126818">this paper</url>, and the effect described
            here (the Oberth effect) is useful for real rockets!</fn>
        </p>
        
        <image source="images/hwimg/hw6/rocket_valley.svg" width="50%">
            <shortdescription>
                A rocket on level ground ahead of a depression that ends at a hill.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
                <p>
                    A rocket is on level ground ahead of a depression to its right. The depression
                    leads to a hill at higher altitude than the one the rocket starts.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The lowest point at the depression is at heigth <m>H</m> below the top of the hill,
                    and the rocket starts at height <m>h</m>, which is less than <m>H</m>, below the hill.
                </p>
            </description>
        </image>

        <p>
            At first glance, it is not obvious why the timing of the impulse would even matter. 
            To have a concrete model for the impulse, let's say that the rocket, of mass <m>M_R</m>, throws a 
            blob of mass <m>m</m> backwards, which ends up moving with speed <m>u</m> relative to the rocket.
        </p>

        <p>
            To avoid any ambiguity: immediately after the blob ejection, someone standing on the skateboard would 
            see the blob traveling away with speed <m>u</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                         Show that the change in the rocket's velocity immediately after the blast
                         is the same regardless of where you fire the blast.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Show that the total change in kinetic energy (rocket + fuel) also does not depend on where 
                        you fire the blast.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Finally, show that the change in kinetic energy of the rocket does depend on where
                        the blast is fired
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        To make sure you're interpreting everything correctly, complete the following sentence:
                        <q>The total kinetic energy of the system increases by <fillin characters="5" />. 
                        Mechanistically (or shall we say chemically?), this extra energy comes from <fillin characters="5" />,
                        so it makes sense that it's a fixed amount regardless of when the rocket fires.
                        However, the resulting increase in rocket kinetic energy can be different, because <fillin characters="5" />.</q>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>